
collision
--------------------------------------------------------------

👉 Collision happens when two different keys produce the same hashCode
and go to the same bucket.
-👉 Collision happens when two different keys produce the same hashCode and
 go to the same bucket.

 real life example:
 Book A → hashCode → Shelf 5
 Book B → hashCode → Shelf 5
 👉 Now both are in same place → COLLISION 💥

 ❓ Why does collision happen?
 1. Limited buckets (space problem)
 HashMap has limited bucket size.

 2. hashCode is not unique
 Different objects can produce same hashCode.
 Example:
 Key1 → 101
 Key2 → 101   ❌ same hashCode

 ⚙️ What problem does collision create?
 Without handling collision:
 ❌ Wrong value returned
 ❌ Data overwrite
 ❌ Slow search (O(n))
 ❌ HashMap becomes useless


 🛠️ How Java handles collision
 In Java HashMap:
 ✔ 1. Linked List (Old method)
 When collision happens:
 Bucket 5:
    Key1 → Value1 → Key2 → Value2 → Key3
 👉 Java checks each one using equals()

 ✔ 2. Balanced Tree (Java 8+ improvement)
 If too many items in same bucket:
 Linked List → becomes Red-Black Tree 🌳

 👉 hashCode = bucket number
 👉 collision = crowd in same bucket
 👉 solution = list or tree to manage crowd

 🌳 When HashMap converts List → Tree (Important Notes)
 HashMap uses Linked List initially for handling collisions
 When too many elements are added in the same bucket, performance becomes slow (O(n))
 To improve performance, Java converts the list into a Red-Black Tree

 🔥 Conversion Conditions (VERY IMPORTANT)
 ⭐ 1. TREEIFY_THRESHOLD
 If number of nodes in a bucket ≥ 8
 Then Linked List → Tree
 bucket size ≥ 8 → convert to Tree

 ⭐ 2. MIN_TREEIFY_CAPACITY
 HashMap size must be ≥ 64
 Otherwise resizing happens instead of tree conversion
 if (capacity < 64) → resize
 if (capacity ≥ 64) → treeify

 ⭐ 3. UNTREEIFY_THRESHOLD
 If nodes reduce to ≤ 6
 Tree → back to Linked List
 bucket size ≤ 6 → convert back to List

 🧠 Why these values?
 8 → balance between memory & performance
 64 → avoid unnecessary tree creation for small maps
 6 → avoid frequent switching (optimization)
